---
description: Do not leave a sentence that only announces a count
alwaysApply: true
---

# No count-only sentences

A sentence that only announces a count does not stay. `There are 14 of them.` and `It looked at four things.` are the shape. The next sentence already says what the things are, so the count line can go and take no claim with it.

Fold the number into the sentence that does the work. `They are 14 bugs that can happen in those operations, such as setting up a call or releasing a device.`

This is not a ban on short sentences. A sentence under 15 words is a recovery point, and `.readability.py` asks for them. `Both versions follow it.` stays, because it states the fairness control. `There is no hypervisor.` stays, because it states a fact about the host. Judge each hit. Do not sweep every short sentence.

`.aitells.py` reports the four-word shape as `counting stub`. That report is a list to read, not a gate. The same defect also appears in longer lines, such as `There are 14 of them.`, which the four-word check does not catch.
